<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xia dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2070–1600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Xiachao, Hsia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xia dynasty is the first dynasty in traditional Chinese historiography, said to have been founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a> after Shun, the last of the Five Emperors, gave him the throne. No contemporaneous records of the Xia survive; the earliest mentions are in the oldest chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, from the early Western Zhou period. Traditional chronology places it between 2205 and 1766 BCE, while the Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project proposed 2070 to 1600 BCE. Many Western historians consider it unsubstantiated, but most Chinese archaeologists identify it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture</a>. Tradition holds that its last king, Jie, was overthrown by Tang, founder of the Shang dynasty.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Traditional accounts trace the dynasty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>. His father Gun had been appointed by Emperor Yao to stop the flooding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and failed after nine years of building levees. Shun then appointed Yu, who organised people from different tribes to dig channels in the major rivers and lead the water out to the sea. Afterwards he led an army against the Sanmiao tribe and exiled it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> area.</p><p>Shun, judging Yu worthy, gave him the throne. Near the end of his life Yu passed it to his son Qi rather than to the most capable candidate, which set the precedent for hereditary rule. Zhenxun and Yangcheng are believed to have been two of the capitals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, the third king, Tai Kang, was an avid hunter and an ineffective ruler. The Bamboo Annals report that while he hunted beyond the Luo River, Hou Yi, leader of the Youqiong tribe, attacked Zhenxun, beginning a long interregnum. Hou Yi was killed by his former chief minister Han Zhuo, whose forces later killed King Xiang and took the throne. Xiang's son Shao Kang survived as a fugitive, gathered local lords who hated Han Zhuo and defeated him. His reign and that of his son Zhu are remembered as among the most prosperous.</p><p>The last king, Jie, is described as immoral and tyrannical, as many final rulers are in Chinese historiography. Tang overthrew him and founded the Shang dynasty, and is said to have given the remnants of the Xia clan the small state of Qi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>No contemporaneous records of the Xia exist. The earliest mentions are in the oldest chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, which report speeches of the early Western Zhou and liken the Zhou conquest of the Shang to the Shang succession of the Xia. In the 1920s Gu Jiegang and the Doubting Antiquity School were the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to question the traditional story systematically, and some historians suggest the Zhou invented the Xia as a pretext. Others cite a bronze bell of Shu Yi, a descendant of the Shang, whose inscription says Tang overthrew the Xia.</p><p>Xu Xusheng's survey of 1959 found the urban site of Erlitou near Yanshi. Carbon dating of 2005 and 2006 placed Erlitou at 1735 to 1530 BCE. Most Chinese archaeologists identify it with the Xia, while many Western archaeologists consider the identification unprovable, since the traditional accounts offer too little testable detail. Chronologies differ: Liu Xin's tradition gives 2205 to 1766 BCE and the state-commissioned Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project 2070 to 1600 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xia dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese scholars generally identify it with the <b>Xia dynasty</b>, a link others think unproven without written evidence.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
